:: suitor_date_aldren_1 [suitor-date aldren]

The Royal Garden in morning light was Edran Mossfoot's cathedral. Every path was a nave, every rose bed an altar, and the old gardener himself was always somewhere among the blooms — barefoot, dirt-fingered, communing with the earth in a language that required no words.

Sir Aldren Thornwild waited at the garden entrance.

<<passage_img "character/aldren/aldren_3.png">><</passage_img>>

He stood in polished sun-forged armour — ceremonial, not combat, the metal worked with the golden sunburst of his house — with a flowing white cape that caught the breeze and held it like a sail. His hair was the colour of late summer wheat: thick, wavy, falling to his collar, framing a face that was handsome in the way that old paintings depicted heroes. Strong jaw, golden skin, green eyes that crinkled at the edges when he smiled.

He smiled now.

"Your Grace." He bowed — deep, unhurried, the cape sweeping the flagstones. When he rose, his eyes met mine with a warmth that contained nothing I had feared. No mockery. No pity. No discomfort at the visible evidence of what I was becoming. He looked at the tunic-dress, at the braid, at the softer line of my jaw, and he simply... saw me. Whoever I was today. Exactly that.

"Sir Aldren." I inclined my head in the formal greeting Serelyne had drilled into me — chin down, not forward; acknowledge, don't defer. "I trust the morning finds you well."

"Better now," he said, and the earnestness of it was so naked that I nearly laughed. There was no artifice in this man. He was warmth incarnate — golden through and through — and his sincerity was either the most disarming weapon I had ever encountered or no weapon at all.

We walked. The garden's central path led through Edran's rose beds — crimson, ivory, pale blush, deep violet — and the scent was thick enough to taste. Morning dew still clung to the petals, catching the light in prismatic droplets that scattered tiny rainbows across the flagstones.

Edran was there, of course. Kneeling among the white roses, his bare feet caked with dark earth, his long brown hair tangled with leaves. He looked up as we passed — looked at Aldren, looked at me, and a knowing expression crossed his weathered face.

Without a word, he reached into the bed and plucked a single white rose. Held it out to Aldren with dirt-blackened fingers and an expression that said everything words would have ruined.

Aldren accepted the rose. Turned to me. Presented it with a half-bow that was pure storybook romance — the kind of gesture that should have been ridiculous, corny, the stuff of ballads written by bards who had never actually been in love.

His sincerity sold it utterly.

"For the heir," he said. "Whatever form the heir chooses."

I took the rose. The thorns had been trimmed — Edran's doing, no doubt — and the stem was smooth against my reshaped fingers. The petals were cool and impossibly soft, and the scent was clean and sharp, like snowmelt.

"Thank you." My voice came out quieter than I intended.

We walked on.

[[Continue the garden walk|suitor_date_aldren_2]]


:: suitor_date_aldren_2 [suitor-date aldren]

The conversation began where most conversations begin when two people are acutely aware that they are being observed and assessed: with careful, navigable territory.

Aldren spoke of his childhood in the Thornwild estates. The lands were green and vast, he said — rolling hills that turned amber in autumn, forests that bordered the realm's northern reaches. His father had been a border knight, tall and laughing and gone too soon.

"He fell in the Border Wars," Aldren said, his voice steady but softer. "I was seven. My mother raised me after — alone, with no title of her own, only the estate and her stubbornness." He glanced at me. "She taught me everything I know about honour. And about stubbornness."

A mirror. Whether he knew it or not — and I suspected, looking at his open, unguarded face, that he did not — he was holding up a mirror. A child raised by a single parent of extraordinary will. A father shaped more by absence than presence. A household where duty and love were the same word spoken in different tones.

"My father is still alive," I said, surprising myself with the honesty. "But he has always been... peripheral. The Queen is the centre. Everything orbits her. Therion exists in her shadow by choice, by design, by the simple gravitational truth that Alinora is the sun and the rest of us are objects in her sky."

Aldren nodded. Not with pity — with recognition. "My mother was the same. A sun. And I was content to be warmed by her, rather than burned by my father's fire." He paused. "I think perhaps the children of suns learn to shine differently."

We had reached the far end of the rose walk, where the garden path descended through a stone archway toward the lakeshore. The morning mist had not yet burned away, and the lake was a sheet of hammered silver beneath a pale sky. Somewhere across the water, a heron called — a raw, prehistoric sound that cut through the garden's cultivated peace.

Aldren's armour clinked softly as he walked. Not the heavy, crashing noise of battle plate, but a musical sound, like wind chimes forged in a smithy. Every piece had been fitted to his frame with a craftsman's precision, and he moved in it as naturally as I moved in the tunic-dress.

"I believe a sword protects — it does not define," he said, as though continuing a thought that had begun somewhere before this conversation. "I have seen men whose entire identity was sheathed at their hip, and when they lost the blade, they lost themselves with it. A knight should be a knight without armour, without weapon, without title. The protection should live here—" He tapped his chest. "Not here." He tapped the pommel of his ceremonial sword.

It was the most articulate thing any suitor had said to me, and he delivered it without flourish, without the sense that he was performing. He was simply telling me what he believed, and trusting me to decide whether it mattered.

"May I ask you something?" he said.

"You may."

He stopped walking. Turned to face me. The green eyes were serious now — not grave, not solemn, but serious in the way of a man who was about to say something he had been thinking about and had decided to say simply rather than cleverly.

"Does it hurt?"

Four words. No preamble. No elaborate lead-in about the honour of transformation or the sacred duty of the crown or any of the courtly circumlocutions I had grown to loathe. Just — does it hurt.

No one had asked me that so simply.

Serelyne spoke of the transformation in terms of progress and protocol. Thalara described it clinically — tissue development, hormonal cascades, skeletal remodelling. Maera avoided the subject with a dancer's skill, circling it in conversation the way she circled obstacles in the corridors. Even Aldric addressed it only in terms of its impact on my fighting stance.

No one had simply asked if it hurt.

[[Answer honestly|suitor_date_aldren_3]]


:: suitor_date_aldren_3 [suitor-date aldren]

"Yes."

The word came out before I could curate it. Raw and unpolished and exactly the thing Serelyne would have wanted me not to say.

"It hurts constantly," I continued, and the honesty was a dam breaking — weeks of compressed silence cracking open in the presence of a man who had asked the one question no one else had thought to ask. "Not always sharply. Sometimes it is a dull ache — here—" I pressed my palm flat against my chest, against the budding tissue beneath the linen, and felt the soreness throb in response to the pressure. "The growing. The tissue pushing outward against muscle and rib and skin that were not designed for it. It is like — have you ever broken a bone?"

"My collarbone," he said. "When I was twelve."

"The ache of bone knitting. That deep, grinding tenderness that tells you something is happening beneath the surface that you cannot stop and cannot accelerate. That is what the chest feels like. Every day. Worse in the morning, when the potions are freshest."

We had started walking again — slowly, along the lakeshore path where the stones were smooth and the mist curled around our ankles. Aldren walked beside me, not ahead, his pace matched to mine.

"And the rest of it," I said, because the dam was broken and I could not seem to rebuild it. "The disorientation. I reach for a doorframe and miss because my shoulders are narrower than my memory insists. I turn too quickly and the balance is wrong — the weight distribution has shifted, the hips are wider, the centre of gravity is lower than it was a month ago. I am living in a house whose rooms rearrange themselves every night, and every morning I wake and have to learn the layout again."

Aldren was quiet. Not uncomfortable quiet — listening quiet. The kind of silence that a person maintains not because they have nothing to say but because they understand that the most important thing they can do right now is hold space for someone else's truth.

"And the grief," I said, quieter now. "That is the part no one mentions. The grief of losing the body you knew. I was not — I was not in love with my old body. I did not worship my own reflection. But it was mine. Every scar, every callus, every proportion was a map of my life, and the potions are redrawing it. And there are days when I catch myself reaching for a muscle that is no longer there, or looking in the mirror and seeing a face that is almost mine but not quite, and the grief of that — the strange, untidy grief of becoming someone you have not yet learned to be — that is what hurts the most."

I stopped walking. Realised my eyes were burning.

Aldren stood beside me on the lakeshore path. The mist swirled between us. The heron called again from across the water — long and mournful and ancient.

"Thank you," he said.

"For what?"

"For trusting me with that." He said it simply. Without grandiosity. A man who understood that honesty was a gift and treated it as such.

The silence between us was not awkward. It was the silence of two people who had unexpectedly arrived somewhere real, and who needed a moment to acknowledge the terrain before continuing.

"My mother," Aldren said, after a time, "used to skip stones on the lake near our estate. She said patience is a weapon, and the lake is your sparring partner." He looked at the water. "Shall we?"

[[Skip stones with Aldren|suitor_date_aldren_4]]


:: suitor_date_aldren_4 [suitor-date aldren]

He was surprisingly good at it.

The first stone left his hand in a low, flat arc — wrist snapping at the release point with the controlled precision of a man who had been throwing things accurately since childhood. The stone kissed the water once, twice, three times, four, five, six — six skips before it lost momentum and slipped beneath the silver surface with barely a ripple.

"Six," he said, grinning. The grin transformed his face from handsome to boyish, the green eyes crinkling, the carefully maintained courtly composure dissolving into something genuine and warm and utterly disarming.

I selected a stone. Flat, smooth, palm-sized — the kind Torin and I used to throw when we were boys, competing for skips on the river behind the castle. I set my feet, drew back my arm, and threw.

The motion felt wrong.

Not catastrophically wrong. Not painful. But the arm that threw the stone was not the arm that had thrown stones a year ago. The shoulder was narrower, the joint socket slightly repositioned, and the whip-crack of the release that had once come naturally required a conscious adjustment I had not anticipated. The stone left my hand at an angle — too steep, too high — and instead of skipping, it plunged into the lake with a hollow *plonk* and vanished.

"Ah," I said.

Aldren did not laugh. He did not comment on the miss with careful, patronising encouragement. He simply picked up another stone and held it out.

"The elbow," he said. "May I?"

I nodded. He stepped behind me — close enough that I could smell him, soap and leather and something warm beneath that might have been his skin — and placed his fingers on my elbow. The touch was brief and professional. A trainer's correction, not a suitor's advance. He adjusted the angle of the joint — inward by perhaps two degrees — and stepped back.

"Try again."

I threw. The stone left my hand cleanly this time, the adjusted elbow angle compensating for the shifted shoulder geometry, and it skipped — once, twice, three times — before sinking.

"Three," I said. "Half of yours."

"Three with a body you are still learning," he said. "When you have finished learning it, you will beat me."

There was something in the way he said it — *you will beat me* — that was neither flirtation nor flattery. It was an assessment. A knight's assessment of another person's potential. And it landed in my chest with a warmth that had nothing to do with the morning sun.

We skipped stones for perhaps twenty minutes. The mist burned off. The lake turned from silver to blue. My throws improved — the body's new mechanics clicking into place through repetition, the way Aldric's combat drills had taught the feminine guard through a thousand iterations.

Aldren told me about his mother. How she had governed the estate alone after his father's death, managing tenants and harvests and disputes with a combination of iron will and genuine compassion that earned her a loyalty no lord had managed. How she had taught him that a sword protects — it does not define. How she had died three years ago, in her sleep, with Aldren holding her hand, and how the estate now was his but felt emptier without the sound of her humming in the kitchen.

I told him about Therion. About the man who painted and played music while his wife ruled a kingdom. About the strange, quiet courage of choosing gentleness in a world that rewarded only strength. About the evenings when Therion would sit with me in the garden — this garden, these paths — and say nothing, and how the nothing he said was more nourishing than all of Serelyne's lectures combined.

The conversation was easy. Warm. Like sitting beside a fire that gave heat without smoke.

And throughout it all — throughout the stone-skipping and the stories and the morning light shifting across the water — I was aware of my body. The ache in my chest. The way the tunic-dress moved against new curves. The unfamiliar weight at my hips. The softness of my hands. And Aldren saw it all, and said nothing about it, and treated me as though I were exactly the person I was supposed to be at this exact moment, which was the most generous thing anyone had done for me in weeks.

[[Walk back to the garden gate|suitor_date_aldren_5]]


:: suitor_date_aldren_5 [suitor-date aldren]

We walked back through the garden. The roses had opened further in the warming air — the crimson ones wide and velvety, the white ones luminous, the pale blush ones blushing deeper. Edran had vanished, as he always did, melting back into the greenery like a spirit of the garden itself.

Aldren walked beside me. Our shoulders were nearly level — his slightly broader, mine slightly narrower, the difference smaller than it would have been a month ago and larger than it would be a month hence. The space between us was comfortable. Warm. Steady. A space that said: I am here. I am not going anywhere. Take as long as you need.

The garden gate appeared. Wrought iron, laced with climbing roses, the point where the cultivated beauty of the garden met the structured stone of the castle's outer wall. The transition between Edran's world and Serelyne's.

Aldren stopped.

He turned to face me. The green eyes were soft and serious and very, very present. The morning light was behind him, catching the gold of his hair like a halo in a temple fresco, and I was acutely aware that this was the moment — the climax of the date, the point at which a suitor would reveal something of their true intentions before retreating.

He knelt.

Not performatively. Not with the theatrical flourish of a man who had rehearsed the gesture before a mirror. He simply — sank. One knee to the ground, cape pooling around him like spilled cream, head bowed, and the motion was so natural, so devoid of pretension, that it took me a moment to understand what was happening.

He reached for my hand.

My hand. The hand the potions had been reshaping — the tapered fingers, the smaller palm, the wrist that was more bone and less muscle than it had been. He took it gently, as though he were handling something fragile and precious, and turned it so the knuckles faced upward.

He pressed his lips to my knuckles.

The sensation rippled up my arm and into my chest and settled behind the budding tissue with a warmth that was entirely different from the ache I had grown accustomed to. His lips were dry and warm and the kiss lasted perhaps two seconds — long enough to be felt, brief enough to be chaste, precise enough to communicate something that words would have cluttered.

He raised his head. Looked up at me from one knee.

"Whatever you become," he said, "I would be honoured to stand beside it."

He rose. Bowed. His eyes held mine for one more breath — steady, golden, unafraid — and then he turned and walked away, the white cape catching the morning breeze, his armour chiming that soft, musical chime, his footsteps sure and unhurried on the garden path.

I stood at the gate.

The white rose was in my left hand. The ghost of his lips lingered on the knuckles of my right — knuckles that were no longer a man's, not yet a woman's, belonging to someone in between who was trembling and could not entirely explain why.

[[How did that make you feel?|suitor_date_aldren_6]]


:: suitor_date_aldren_6 [suitor-date aldren]

I pressed my back against the garden gate. The wrought iron was cool through the linen of the tunic-dress. The white rose trembled in my grip.

My heart was doing something complicated. Not racing — Aldren did not provoke racing. He provoked something slower and deeper, a gravitational pull, a warmth that settled into the bones and stayed. The kind of feeling you did not notice until you tried to move away from it and found it had already rooted.

The ghost of his lips on my knuckles. The sound of his armour chiming as he walked away. The simple, devastating honesty of *whatever you become.*

Not *whoever.* Whatever. As though he understood — truly understood — that what I was becoming was not merely a change of person but a change of category, a metamorphosis so fundamental that the usual pronouns and frameworks did not apply, and he had chosen the word that held the most space.

I looked down at my hands. The right one, where his lips had been, was trembling. The left one held the rose so tightly that the smooth stem was denting the soft flesh of my palm.

