Not with the wild, stammering terror of infatuation — but with something quieter and more dangerous. The slow, warm recognition of a person who might understand. Who might see the body in transition and the soul in flux and choose to stay not because of what I would become but because of the becoming itself.

I brought the rose to my face and breathed in. Snowmelt. New beginnings.

The garden gate at my back. The castle ahead. And somewhere between the two, the fading chime of golden armour carried on the morning wind.

