Too forward.

He had touched me. Without waiting for me to initiate — Serelyne's explicit instruction — he had taken my hand and pressed his mouth to my skin, and however gentle the gesture, however correct the kneeling posture, it was a presumption. A claim made in the language of chivalry, dressed in courtesy, but a claim nonetheless.

My knuckles tingled where his lips had been. I wiped them against the tunic-dress and felt nothing but irritation.

Or so I told myself as I walked away from the gate with the white rose still gripped in fingers that would not unclench.

